# Copy to .env.local for local development.
#
# NEVER put a real secret in this file — it is committed. Values here are empty
# placeholders and comments only.

# --- Database ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Postgres connection string used by the app at runtime. Point it at a pooled
# connection in production. The auth tables (users, sessions, accounts,
# verifications, api_tokens) live in this same database as the log tables.
DATABASE_URL=

# The DIRECT (unpooled) connection, used only by `prisma migrate`. A transaction
# pooler cannot hold the advisory lock migrations take, so this must bypass it.
# Locally the two are usually the same value.
DIRECT_DATABASE_URL=

# --- Local development without signing in ------------------------------------
# DANGER: these two make getSession() return a mock ADMIN session to EVERY
# visitor - no sign-in, full access to every application's logs, and the ability
# to mint and revoke ingest keys. They are commented out deliberately: this file
# is copied wholesale into deployment environments, and isAuthSkipped() has no
# NODE_ENV guard of its own.
#
# Uncomment ONLY in .env.local, on your own machine. A production build refuses
# to boot with either set - see src/constants/environment.ts.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ is needed alongside the plain variable because client bundles
# cannot read the plain one.
# SKIP_AUTH=true
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SKIP_AUTH=true

# --- Auth (required once SKIP_AUTH is off) -----------------------------------
# The signing secret for session cookies. REQUIRED in production and at least
# 32 characters: src/instrumentation.ts refuses to start the server otherwise,
# rather than fall back to a shared default that would let anyone mint a
# session. Generate one with:
#   openssl rand -base64 32
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=

# THIS APP'S OWN ORIGIN, e.g. https://logger.example.com - never a third party's.
# It is the base every /api/auth/* URL, the trusted origins and every OAuth
# callback are built from. REQUIRED in production as an https origin with no
# path, query or fragment: the server refuses to start without it, because the
# alternative is deriving all three from each request's Host header, which a
# caller chooses. Leave it unset in development and the origin is derived from
# the request; cleartext http is tolerated only on a loopback host.
BETTER_AUTH_URL=

# --- Reverse proxy -----------------------------------------------------------
# How many reverse proxies in front of the app append to X-Forwarded-For and
# may be trusted - usually 1 (one Caddy or nginx). The sign-in and ingest
# throttles read the client address that many entries from the RIGHT, because
# the first entry is whatever the client sent. Leave it unset when nothing sits
# in front of the app: the per-address throttles then stand down rather than
# key on an address a caller chose, and a production server warns at start.
# Vercel is detected and trusted as 1.
TRUSTED_PROXY_HOPS=

# --- Google sign-in (OPTIONAL) -----------------------------------------------
# Leave BOTH empty to run on email and password alone: the provider is only
# registered when the pair is complete, and the sign-in page only offers the
# button when it is. Setting one without the other is treated as "not
# configured" rather than as a half-working button.
#
# The authorised redirect URI to register with Google is:
#   ${BETTER_AUTH_URL}/api/auth/callback/google
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=

# --- Cron --------------------------------------------------------------------
# A token minted on the dashboard's API tokens page (/tokens, administrators
# only). Vercel Cron sends it as `Authorization: Bearer <value>` to
# /api/cron/retention. Set it in the Vercel dashboard, never here.
CRON_SECRET=

# --- Provisioning the first account ------------------------------------------
# There is no sign-up, deliberately, on either the password or the Google
# route: any signed-in account reads every application's logs, so registration
# is not self-service. Create the first administrator from the command line:
#   pnpm --filter @stuffs/logger-dashboard user:create-admin -- \
#     --email you@example.com --name "Your Name"
# It prints a temporary password once. Everyone after that is managed on the
# dashboard's Users page.
